  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Break Free! Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CEFMU, teenage pregnancy (TP) and female genital mutilation/cutting (FGM/C) are widespread in Amhara, Ethiopia and linked to multiple social, economic, religious and cultural drivers. Key contributing factor to CEFMU and FGM/C is the lack of sufficient law enforcement and monitoring of this practice.
The prevention of CEFMU and Teenage Pregnancies (TP) is more urgent than ever, as COVID-19 situation has dramatically changed the context in which adolescent girls live resulting in increasing levels of GBV, increasing unpaid care work, rising sexual exploitation, decreased access to education and increasing rates of CEFMU and TP. This crisis has further isolated and excluded adolescent girls in remote areas from accessing SRHR information and services, especially concerning contraception and other essential SRH services. Lockdown restrictions and enforcement of COVID-19 prevention methods have led to an increase in harmful practices such as CEFMU and FGM/C, reducing the effectiveness of referral pathways for essential services, and resulting in poorer SRH outcomes for girls. The loss of employment and income by parents and caregivers is expected to increase girls&#x2019; school dropout and girls&#x2019; vulnerability to sexual exploitation, resulting in TP and Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs), including HIV. This economic downturn will likely have a large impact on the poverty levels in Ethiopia where CEFMU is most prevalent ( 40%). Because poverty is a key driver of CEFMU, these economic impacts are anticipated to increase rates of CEFMU and TP in vulnerable communities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Empowering adolescents to claim their SRHR rights, supported by CS, will be achieved if  CSOs, youth-led groups and networks are strengthened to lobby and advocate for improved legislation and to promote social norm change in favour of the prevention of CEFMU/TP. 
Education opportunities and a community supportive of girls&#x2019; education will stimulate adolescent girls at risk of TP/CEFMU to access and remain in school, while promoting the re-entrance of girls who have discontinued school due to pregnancy. In order to create a supportive and improved socio-cultural environment that promotes education for girls and SRHR for young people, relevant gate keepers, teachers and parents will be made aware of the importance of adopting attitudes favourable to keeping girls in school. 
Supporting CSOs to organise, participate and communicate enables CSOs to claim better education and SRHR outcomes for girls and young women, and influence decision-making processes. Duty bearers and decision-makers are made aware of and encouraged to take action to fulfil the education needs of girls based on the effective lobby and advocacy of civil society. Decision-makers at local, national and regional level will be sensitized to build linkages between the levels resulting that civil society is able to elevate issues to higher decision-making levels, and vice versa, they can hold lower-level policy makers and duty bearers accountable for implementing decisions taken at higher levels. This will be enabled by establishing participation and accountability mechanisms between decision-makers/duty bearers and civil society with the support of enhanced youth agency.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUMMARY
Millions of young people don&#x2019;t have access to appropriate, gender-responsive, quality SRHR education, information and services. This makes them vulnerable to unintended pregnancies, often followed by child marriage (CM). The Break Free! programme empowers adolescents and young people to make their own free and informed choices to speak out and have control over their SRH and lives. BF! is tackling root causes of gender inequality,  unequal power relations and discriminatory social norms, systems, structures, policies and practices, supported by civil society and improved legislation. The project will be implemented between 2021 and 2025 by the Break Free! Consortium consisting of Plan International Netherlands, SAT and FAWE, technical partners KIT and RMT in collaboration with Netherlands&#x2019; Embassies. It will be carried out in Ethiopia, Kenya, Sudan, Zambia, Malawi, Mozambique, Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger and a regional Pan-African component to sustain the country programmes.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The programme is a.o. based upon the results and findings of the YID programme (2015-2020), implemented by Plan in Ethiopia, combined with experience of FAWE and SAT&#x2019;s programmes on this theme. All partners have experience on improving legislation and policies on CEFMU and implementation thereof, through L&amp;A and enhancing adolescent&#x2019;s skills to claim their SRH rights, quality of SRH education, information and service provision. This resulted a.o. into the development of the Southern Africa Development Community (SADC) Model Law on Eradicating Child Marriage, served as legal framework for national law and policy-makers and was used as civil society (CS) advocacy tool across the SADC region. 
While Ethiopia has made great strides since April 2018 in the opening of the &#x2018;civic space&#x2019;, amid continued insecurity and uncertainty the potential for relapse remains. The opening of media space allows for potential collaboration between media organisations and CSOs on community-focused human rights sensitisation. Still, the CS sector needs to re-establish linkages for effective collaboration on key advocacy projects and the number of organised CSOs and particularly youth-led CSOs in Ethiopia is still limited.
Ethiopian government has demonstrated a high level of commitment to addressing HTPs, however, implementation of policies and strategies is hampered by limited coordination and information-sharing as well as limited capacity and resources. Ethiopia does not have explicit laws or policies in place that guarantee pregnant girls or adolescent mothers the right to go back to school, leading to irregular re-entry policies and practices at school level.</narrative>
  </description>
